  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 AM MST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nNorthwest flow continues across the region today. Looking at \nmainly light precipitation across the central mountains around \nStanley and the eastern highlands for most of the day. Late this\nafternoon and tonight, a larger push of moisture associated \nwith a cold front will arrive. This will produce the best \nchances for widespread precipitation. Precipitation will end \nSaturday morning with drier air working in behind the front. The\nStanley area should see the most precipitation, perhaps even \nexceeding an inch of liquid from this system. The rest of the \ncentral mountains are looking at 0.25 to 0.50 inches. The \neastern highlands will have similar numbers with amounts \napproaching an inch along the Wyoming border. The Snake River \nPlain and Magic Valley will probably see 0.10 inches or less. \nMost of the significant snowfall is up high, above 7000 feet, \nand as a whole not overly impactful. The lower elevations down \nto 6000 feet could see a couple inches. Galena Summit could see \nup to 6 inches. Teton Pass in Wyoming (RIW's area) will have \nimpressive amounts. Expect strong gusty winds along the front \nlate tonight with gusts over 40 mph likely across much of east \nIdaho. The eastern Magic Valley has at least a 50 percent chance\nof gusts exceeding 45 mph (criteria level). So will likely \nissue a wind advisory there for late tonight into Saturday \nmorning to catch the peak winds. \n\nWe should see a break Saturday afternoon through most of Sunday \nbefore our next round of precipitation arrives. The system is more \nwesterly instead of northwesterly so the shadow effect on the Snake \nRiver Plain from the central mountains shouldn't be as pronounced. \nLiquid amounts with that system will likely range from 0.15 to 0.40 \ninches form the mountains and highlands with up to 0.10 inches \nacross the Snake River Plain and Magic Valley. This system does not \nlinger long and is gone by Monday NIght. Snowfall amounts with this \nsystem are mainly above 6500 feet and in the 1-3 inch range.",
